definition:
    The fissile star of natural uranium — just 0.72% of the metal, yet the only isotope found in nature that sustains chain reactions easily. Its nucleus deforms and splits when it captures a slow neutron, releasing ~200 MeV plus fresh neutrons. With a 704-million-year half-life it still accounts for nearly all of Earth's remaining U-235; the Oklo deposit in Gabon burned some naturally two billion years ago when concentrations ran higher.
